  "https://www.architecturaldigest.com/story/european-union-set-bold-new-brussels-headquarters": {
    "url": "https://www.architecturaldigest.com/story/european-union-set-bold-new-brussels-headquarters",
    "fetched": true,
    "http_status": "200",
    "text": "The European Union is Set For a Bold New Brussels Headquarters | Architectural Digest Skip to main content AD PRO DIRECTORY APPLY NOW Architectural Digest Architectural Digest DIRECTORY ARCHITECTURE + DESIGN SHOPPING CELEBRITY AD IT YOURSELF AD PRO Menu Menu Search Menu Menu Directory Architecture + Design Shopping Chevron Celebrity AD It Yourself AD Pro Chevron Real Estate Lifestyle MORE VIDEO THE MAGAZINE NEWSLETTERS Search Search Architectural Digest Search Close Menu Menu Menu Architecture The European Union Is Set for a Bold New Brussels Headquarters Belgian, Italian, and British architects collaborated on the EU’s innovative new home By Carrie Hojnicki Photography by Philippe Samyn Architects & Engineers/Europa December 19, 2016 An exterior view of the new European Union headquarters. Save Story Save this story Save Story Save this story Come January, the European Union will have a new headquarters in its home city of Brussels. The state-of-the-art space, which was designed by Belgian firm Samyn and Partners in conjunction with Italian architects Studio Valle and British engineers BuroHappold, was first conceived to better accommodate the ten new states that were inducted in 2004, the largest single addition in the EU’s history. The new building will occupy what was formerly an apartment complex known as the Residence Palace, which sits conveniently next to the EU’s original home (the two will soon be connected by a footbridge). The design is contemporary at its finest, with a soaring glass-paneled façade that encloses a mega-atrium, through which one can find offices, conference spaces, and a dining area. Perhaps most notable about the exterior, however, is its sustainable nature: It comprises 3,750 recycled window frames sourced from demolitions across the continent. At night the ball-shaped volume that contains the majority of the building’s functional spaces lights up, casting an ethereal glow. A look inside the new $334 million EU headquarters. The long road to the building’s completion has been a thorn in many EU members’ sides. The building was initially expected to be finished in 2012 for some $83 million less than its final cost of nearly $334 million. Explore Architecture Read More Architecture 111 West 57th Street: Everything You Need to Know About the World’s Skinniest Skyscraper The second tallest residential tower in the Western Hemisphere, this New York City landmark blends cutting-edge technology and historic preservation By Rachel Davies The Report 5 Things Nate Berkus and Jeremiah Brent Learned While Renovating Their House in Portugal Proof that even a couple of old pros can pick up something new with every project By Ingrid Abramovitch Set Design Go Behind the Scenes of Deborah Vance’s Casino on Hacks The finale episode of the HBO comedy was a sweet and funny farewell to our favorite fictional comedian, but we didn’t spend nearly enough time inside her new Art Deco palace By Rachel Wallace Shopping 8 Modern Farmhouse Airbnbs for a Rustic Getaway If you can’t make that modern farmhouse reno happen, these are the next best thing By Kristine Hansen Architecture + Design Surrealist Design Enhances the Picassos in this Paris Apartment Cocteau meets Eileen Gray in this collectors’ apartment overlooking the rooftops of the French capital By Fanny Guénon des Mesnards Architecture + Design It’s Always Spring In This Milan Apartment Abundant natural light and vintage details lend this 2,700-square-foot pad a verdant vibe By Ludovica Stevan Architecture + Design A Craft-Forward Restoration of a 19th-Century Schoolhouse Channels the Uninhibited Spirit of an Old Circus In a converted schoolhouse in a small Vermont town, studio Pat Austin spent four patient years creating a playful retreat for the head of Donkey Milk Studios By Keith Flanagan Shopping Your Home Needs an Enormous Clay Pot A rare ceramic piece is all the rage, as evidenced by Jeremiah Brent and Nate Berkus’s Portuguese farmhouse By Julia 