  "https://www.constructionnews.co.uk/contracts/mclaren-confirmed-on-132m-oxford-street-revamp-15-02-2024/": {
    "url": "https://www.constructionnews.co.uk/contracts/mclaren-confirmed-on-132m-oxford-street-revamp-15-02-2024/",
    "fetched": true,
    "http_status": "200",
    "text": "McLaren confirmed on £132m Oxford Street revamp | Construction News Login / Register   Menu   Menu   Sign In Subscribe   Construction News Read UK Construction Industry News, Analysis, Opinion and data Home News Financial Legal Buildings Supply Chain Civils Building safety Top UK contractors Technology HS2 Consultants People Moves Government Health and Safety Mental health Skills Brexit Contracts Sustainability M&A Long reads Features Project Reports Interviews Opinion Podcast CN Intelligence Top UK contractors Balfour Beatty Bouygues Bovis Costain Galliford Try ISG Kier Laing O’Rourke Morgan Sindall Mace Multiplex Royal Bam Sir Robert McAlpine Skanska Tilbury Douglas Vinci VolkerWessels Wates Willmott Dixon Ask CN Events Jobs Sponsored Subscribe You are here: Contracts McLaren confirmed on £132m Oxford Street revamp 15 Feb 2024 By Charlotte Banks CGI of refurbished former House of Fraser store McLaren Construction has been selected to refurbish and extend the former House of Fraser department store on Oxford Street, central London. McLaren had already started work on the project almost two years ago for client Publica Properties Establishment under a preconstruction services agreement. Erith is undertaking the demolition and reinstatement works, with Severfield providing the structural steel. The 1937 art deco building, which closed in 2022, will be reimagined as a 34,000 square metre mixed-use development, containing offices, retail space and a gym. Work will include demolition on the fifth floor, with 10 steel columns reused for a set-back extension on the eight floor, featuring a restaurant with a terrace area. The other 11 columns – 100 tonnes of steel – will be used on an office retrofit being carried out by Willmott Dixon in east London. Developer FORE Partnership, which is removing the beams, claims this marks the first use of pre-Second World War steel in a UK construction project. McLaren will reconstruct the sixth and seventh floors, adding a new terrace. The contractor will also remove staircases, lift shafts and internal divisions from the two western corners to create larger floorplates. McLaren managing director London and South Darren Gill said: “More of these asset-repurposing projects are coming through to the market. Loading… “These are often familiar buildings in prominent locations. They present an opportunity to improve resilience through a variety of new uses – not just for the building, but for the area too.” The building is currently affected by Regent Street Disease, a phenomenon affecting early 20th century buildings that causes steel frames within masonry cladding corrode. McLaren will repair the Portland-stone facade, reinstate architectural features and replace windows, which have rusting frames. The firm is also currently renovating the former Topshop flagship store on Oxford Street, which will become an IKEA. Gill added: “The construction industry will increasingly need the skills to repurpose historic buildings. It’s essential if we want to balance the creation of quality spaces with reduced carbon footprints. “McLaren’s investment in this expertise is paying off with a raft of high-profile cut-and-carve projects in central London.” 2024-02-15 Charlotte Banks Share Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Email Add to Bookmarks Loading... Sign up for news by email Advertisement Advertisement VIEW THE TOP 100 UK CONSTRUCTION CONTRACTORS WHICH SPECIALIST CONTRACTORS ARE DOING BEST? Who are the best-paid bosses in construction? WHICH CONTRACTORS PAY QUICKEST – AND SLOWEST? Most Popular Most Recent Up to 2,000 workers temporarily kicked off Hinkley Point C site after protest Building material deliveries continue to fall Construction hit by sharpest fall in work since first Covid lockdown Midlands brickwork firm takes step to enter administration A million more construction workers needed, says Skills England Concertus launches £1bn framework after council scraps plan New guidance issued in bid to drive down BSR rejections Cost of Morgan Sindall Scottish campus job rises 45% after contamination find Group Metropolitan turnover soars HRB repair red tape cut could spark fresh confusion, industry warns About Construction News Advertising Privacy Policy Cookies Policy Download The CN app Jobs Subscriptions Terms and conditions DIGITAL EDITIONS Contact us FAQs Style guide Forward Features 2025 Copyright © 2026 EMAP Publishing Limited",
